Blepharitis
Opthalmology & ENT
Erythematous eyelid margins, Eyelash crusting/collarettes, Meibomian gland dysfunction

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-006
Reason for Visit: Acute severe ocular burning, photophobia, and inability to open eyes due to crusting.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute bilateral severe gritty ocular pain, severe photophobia, and eyelids sealed shut upon waking. Urgent assessment was required to definitively rule out sight-threatening bacterial keratitis, corneal ulceration, or acute angle-closure glaucoma, and to provide immediate therapeutic relief and eye clearance prior to the next morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My eyes are burning like fire, they were glued shut this morning, and the light is stabbing my brain."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a sudden, severe exacerbation of a chronic gritty sensation in both eyes. Woke up with eyelids firmly stuck together. Endorses severe burning, stinging, and worsening photophobia. Reports a history of erythematous, swollen, and thickened eyelid margins with noticeable crusting firmly attached at the base of the eyelashes. Notes a frothy appearance of the tears. Denies deep aching eye pain, sudden vision loss, or preceding trauma.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently keeping eyes closed due to severe photophobia.
Social/Safety Concerns: Lives alone, unable to safely navigate the home due to closed eyes.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/75 | HR: 82 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Distressed, sitting in a darkened room, wearing sunglasses indoors.
Physical Exam: Visual acuity is mildly reduced but correctable with blinking. Slit-lamp equivalent examination with ophthalmoscope reveals markedly erythematous eyelid margins bilaterally. Cylindrical dandruff (collarettes) firmly attached at the base of the eyelashes. Palpation of the lid margins reveals clogged, pouting meibomian gland orifices expressing inspissated, toothpaste-like secretions. Conjunctiva is mildly injected but corneas are completely clear without epithelial defects or opacities. Pupils equal and reactive to light.
Environmental Observations: Dusty environment, poor ambient lighting.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Nil active medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Exacerbation of Anterior and Posterior Blepharitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Bacterial Keratitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear corneas without focal white infiltrates or epithelial ulceration.

Acute Angle-Closure Glaucoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral presentation, normal pupillary reactivity, clear corneas (no edema), and absence of severe deep aching pain or nausea.

Viral Conjunctivitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Prominence of lid margin inflammation and collarettes, with only mild conjunctival injection and absence of watery discharge or preauricular lymphadenopathy.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe, bilateral ocular distress characterized by burning, crusting, and photophobia, which mandated an immediate home visit to exclude sight-threatening corneal pathology or acute glaucoma. Clinical examination confirmed the absence of corneal involvement and identified pathognomonic signs of severe anterior and posterior blepharitis (collarettes, meibomian gland dysfunction). Immediate intervention with mechanical debridement (lid hygiene) and topical lubrication was necessary to alleviate severe distress and restore functional vision safely during the after-hours period, avoiding an unnecessary emergency department presentation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Performed mechanical lid scrubs using dilute baby shampoo and warm water during the visit to remove crusts. Prescribed Chloramphenicol 0.5% eye drops QID for 5 days for presumed secondary bacterial overgrowth. Prescribed preservative-free lubricating eye drops hourly.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the chronic meibomian gland dysfunction, apply a hot compress for exactly 5 minutes every morning, immediately followed by firm upward massage of the lower lids. The immediate release of trapped oils will noticeably blur vision for a few seconds (confirming success) and then drastically reduce the gritty sensation, providing rapid positive reinforcement for daily compliance.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 1 week.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if patient develops severe deep aching eye pain, sudden reduction in vision, a visibly white spot on the cornea, or worsening redness that does not respond to treatment.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; condition is chronic and prone to intermittent exacerbations.
Clinical Course: Acute symptoms will improve significantly within 48 hours with strict lid hygiene and lubricants. The underlying condition requires lifelong management.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If your vision suddenly drops, the pain changes to a deep, severe ache, or you see a white spot on the colored part of your eye, go to the emergency department immediately.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The glands in your eyelids are blocked, causing the tears to evaporate too quickly and bacteria to overgrow on the lashes. The hot compresses melt the blocked oils, and the antibiotic drops treat the bacterial overgrowth.
Actionable Steps: Perform the hot compress and lid massage routine every single morning. Use the lubricating drops frequently to flush the eyes.

